mode test

// Regression: field access on a LITERAL base. `"hi".c_str` -- the idiom every
// mode gui example uses for raylib strings -- panicked in the interpreter with
// "field access base is not a place", while the same source compiled fine, so
// the suite and the examples never caught it. It surfaced as `til repl`
// exiting silently on `InitWindow(800, 600, "hi".c_str)`: a literal base is a
// value, not a place, so field_access_base had nothing to point into.
//
// The read is served from a temporary now. That is safe because the
// interpreter INTERNS literal bytes in ctx.builder_str_lit_values and hands
// back a CAP_LIT view of the pooled buffer, so the field word is a pointer
// into the pool, never into the temporary's own three words.
//
// The round trip through from_c_str is the point: it walks to the NUL, which
// caught that the interner malloc'd count+1 bytes for a terminator and never
// wrote it. Compiled literals are C string constants and always terminated,
// so only the interpreter read past the end -- silently, until a literal's
// c_str could actually be reached.

test_literal_c_str_round_trip := test() {
    assert_eq(Str, Str.from_c_str("hello".c_str), "hello")
    assert_eq(Str, Str.from_c_str("".c_str), "")
}
